#show: page.with(
    title: "AI",
    summary: [
        This blog is 100% written by hand
    ],
    header: {
        html.elem("h1", [Written by Hand])
    }
)

I feel like this shouldn't have to be said, but there are many things that I
feel like shouldn't be as they are and this is one of them: Every sentence on
this site is written by hand. I believe that using AI for writing would be
disrespectful to the readers of this site by wasting their time.

I am not an opponent of AI. On the contrary, I use AI nearly every day at work
and on private projects. It's an incredibly useful tool for software engineering
and knowledge work in general. For example, the software powering this blog and
its design have seen many improvements through the help of AI. I also use it to
review what I wrote, and that helps me to correct and improve the texts I write.

So is "written without AI" a lie? I don't believe it is. To me, writing is the
translation of thoughts into words so that another human can understand the
thought. AI is a tool that can help with this by pointing out problems where
this translation process doesn't work, but it's impossible to outsource this
process to an AI.

However, every tool can be used in harmful ways. AI is so easy to misuse, and
the misuse so prevalent, that we have a word for it: _Slop_. Slop can be
generated cheaply (as in not requiring a lot of human time), it looks great at
first glance, and it's often as empty as a glass full of air.

Slop seems to exploit our brain's conditioning to associate a well-written text
with actual thought. At the rate AI is being used today, this conditioning won't
last long and the next generation growing up with Slop will learn not to trust
text implicitly.

Which brings us to why I had to write this. Using AI has a slippery-slope
problem. You start by using a little of it and all of a sudden, you're using it
for everything. This page is not for you, dear reader. This page is to stop me
from using AI for writing by publicly committing to writing by hand.